PUBLIC RESPONSE: {C}

The core distinction is that basket A is a deterministic process (exactly +10% compounded per year with probability 1), while basket B is a random walk around the identical linear trend (ARIMA-with-drift) whose conditional expectation at any fixed horizon therefore also grows at 10% per year. Because expectation is linear, the unconditional expected terminal value after 30 years is identical for both baskets. The 31st-year liquidation is simply a (bounded) stopping time; by the optional-stopping theorem applied to the drift-adjusted process, the expectation remains unchanged. Hence the two strategies have the same expected liquidation proceeds.

Options that claim one basket necessarily dominates (E, H), that one has higher expected value (A, G), or that either process is literally a zero-drift martingale (B, D, F) are false. C is therefore the strongest true statement among the choices.

